rfc help
Command-line tools for working with a log of requests for comments (RFCs).

RFCs are stored as Markdown files in a subdirectory of the project, by default
`docs/rfc`.  Each RFC's file name begins with `RFC-` and its number, such as
`RFC-0001-use-requests-for-comments.md`.  Run "rfc init" to start a log, and
"rfc new" to add to it.

Usage:
  rfc [flags]
  rfc [command]

Available Commands:
  completion  Generate the autocompletion script for the specified shell
  config      Print the configuration in use
  graph       Draw the links between records in Mermaid or Graphviz format
  help        Help about any command
  init        Start a log of RFCs
  link        Record that one RFC obsoletes or updates another
  list        List the RFCs, or write a table of contents
  new         Create the next numbered RFC

Flags:
  -h, --help   help for rfc

Use "rfc [command] --help" for more information about a command.
rfc help new
Creates a new, numbered RFC, in draft.  The title is a single argument, so it
must be quoted if it contains spaces.  The RFC is opened for editing in the
editor specified by the VISUAL or EDITOR environment variable (VISUAL is
preferred; EDITOR is used if VISUAL is not set).  After editing, the file name
of the RFC is output to stdout, so the command can be used in scripts.

If the RFC directory contains a file `templates/template.md`, this is used as
the template for the new RFC.  Otherwise a built-in template is used, with
sections for the summary, motivation, design, drawbacks, alternatives and
unresolved questions.

Multiple --obsolete and --update options can be given, so that the new RFC can
obsolete or update multiple existing RFCs.  An obsoleted RFC loses its Draft
status; an updated one keeps its status.

Usage:
  rfc new TITLE [flags]

Examples:
  # Create a new RFC with the title "Adopt gRPC for internal services":
  rfc new "Adopt gRPC for internal services"

  # Create a new RFC that obsoletes RFC 12:
  rfc new -o 12 "Adopt Connect for internal services"

  # Create a new RFC that obsoletes RFCs 3 and 4, and updates RFC 5:
  rfc new -o 3 -o 4 -u 5 "Version the public API"

Flags:
  -h, --help                 help for new
  -o, --obsolete REFERENCE   a REFERENCE (number or partial filename) to a record this one obsoletes
  -u, --update REFERENCE     a REFERENCE (number or partial filename) to a record this one updates
rfc help link
Records, in both RFCs, that SOURCE obsoletes or updates TARGET.

SOURCE and TARGET are both a reference (number or partial filename) to an RFC.
Exactly one of --obsolete and --update must be given.

With --obsolete, SOURCE replaces TARGET, which is marked "Obsoleted by" SOURCE
and loses its Draft status.

With --update, SOURCE makes substantive changes to TARGET, which is marked
"Updated by" SOURCE and keeps its status.

Usage:
  rfc link (--obsolete | --update) SOURCE TARGET [flags]

Examples:
  # Record that RFC 3 updates RFC 2:
  rfc link --update 3 2

  # Record that RFC 12 obsoletes RFC 10:
  rfc link -o 12 10

Flags:
  -h, --help       help for link
  -o, --obsolete   SOURCE obsoletes TARGET, which loses its Draft status
  -u, --update     SOURCE makes substantive changes to TARGET
rfc nonsense
